export const G6={title:'Instruments I: parts, classification, cutting and grasping instruments (Skill I)',hook:'A general surgery tray can hold more than 100 instruments, and many look alike. A Crile and a Kelly differ only in where the serrations stop; an Adson and a Brown-Adson differ in their teeth. In Skill I you see 15 numbered items and must name each one, spell it correctly and state its purpose. Instrumentation, equipment and supplies is 30% of the test.',objectives:['Name the parts of a ringed instrument and classify instruments by function.','Match knife handles with blades and choose blades for incisions.','Identify scissors and their uses.','Identify tissue forceps by their tips and uses.'],sections:[
['Instrument parts and classification',[
'A ringed instrument such as a hemostat has **finger rings**, **shanks**, a **ratchet** (which locks the instrument closed in steps), a **box lock** (the joint where the two halves cross) and **jaws** with **serrations** (horizontal, longitudinal or cross-hatched) or teeth. Most surgical instruments are made of **stainless steel**; titanium and tungsten carbide inserts (gold-handled instruments) are also used.',
'Instruments are classified by function: **cutting and dissecting** (scalpels, scissors), **grasping and holding** (tissue forceps, Allis, Babcock), **clamping and occluding** (hemostats, bowel clamps), **retracting and exposing** (hand-held and self-retaining retractors), **suturing and stapling** (needle holders), **suctioning** (Yankauer, Frazier, Poole), **dilating and probing** and **viewing** (scopes). Knowing the category helps you guess a new instrument’s purpose.',
"Instrument **trays** are standardized by procedure—a **minor tray**, **laparotomy (major) tray**, **vascular tray**, **orthopedic tray**—and each comes with a **count sheet** listing every instrument. The CST checks the tray against the count sheet during setup and the initial count.",
match('Instrument part',[['Finger rings','Where the user’s fingers go'],['Ratchet','Locks the instrument closed in steps'],['Box lock','Joint where the two halves cross'],['Jaws','Working end that grasps or clamps'],['Serrations','Grooves on the jaws that improve grip'],['Shanks','Section between rings and box lock']])]],
['Knife handles and blades',[
'Knife handles are matched to blades by size. The **No. 3 knife handle** takes **small blades**—**No. 10, 11, 12 and 15**; the **No. 3 long knife handle** takes the same blades for deeper areas; the **No. 7 knife handle** is long and thin (pencil-like) and also takes the small blades for deep or delicate work; the **No. 4 handle** takes **large blades (20–25)**; the **Beaver handle** takes **mini blades** for fine work such as eye and hand surgery.',
'Blade shapes: the **No. 10** has a large curved cutting edge for **skin incisions** and large incisions; the **No. 15** has a **small curved** edge for **short, precise** incisions (such as in plastic surgery or small vessels); the **No. 11** is pointed for stab incisions; the **No. 12** is hooked; the **No. 20** is a larger blade (on a No. 4 handle) for large incisions such as the chest. Blades are loaded and removed **with a needle holder**, never with fingers, and the scalpel is passed using the **neutral zone**.',
match('Handle and blade',[['No. 3 knife handle','Takes blades 10, 11, 12, 15'],['No. 7 knife handle','Long, thin handle for deep or delicate work with small blades'],['No. 4 handle','Takes blades 20–25'],['No. 10 blade','Large curved blade for skin incisions'],['No. 15 blade','Small curved blade for precise incisions'],['No. 20 blade','Large blade for large incisions'],['Beaver handle','Mini blades for fine work'],['No. 3 long handle','Small blades for deeper areas']])]],
['Scissors',[
'**Straight Mayo scissors** are heavy straight scissors used to **cut sutures** (often called **suture scissors**) and sometimes heavy tissue. **Curved Mayo scissors** are heavy, curved scissors used to **cut and dissect heavy or tough tissue** such as **fascia, muscle and the uterus**. **Metzenbaum scissors** are longer and lighter with fine, curved blades for **delicate tissue dissection**; they should never be used to cut sutures, which dulls them.',
'Other scissors include **iris scissors** (small, sharp, for fine work), **Potts scissors** (angled for vascular work), **bandage scissors** (blunt tip for removing dressings) and **wire scissors**. Scissors are passed with the **curved tips pointing in the direction the surgeon will use them** and the rings placed in the surgeon’s palm.',
sort('Which scissors?',['Straight Mayo','Curved Mayo','Metzenbaum'],[['Cutting sutures',0],['Cutting fascia',1],['Dissecting delicate tissue',2],['Heavy curved blades for tough tissue',1],['Long, light, fine curved blades',2],['Often called suture scissors',0]])]],
['Tissue forceps',[
'Tissue forceps (thumb forceps) are held like a pencil. **Plain (smooth) tissue forceps** have smooth tips with fine serrations for **delicate tissue** and dressings. **Adson tissue forceps** are short, fine forceps for **superficial, delicate tissue** such as skin edges in plastic and neurosurgery; **toothed Adson forceps** (1 × 2 teeth) grasp **skin** more securely. **Brown-Adson forceps** have rows of **many fine interlocking teeth** (often 7 × 7) for a gentle, secure grip on skin and soft tissue.',
'**DeBakey forceps** have long, **atraumatic** jaws with fine longitudinal grooves for **vascular and delicate tissue** (blood vessels, bowel)—they grip without crushing. **Bonney forceps** are heavy forceps with **large teeth** for **tough tissue** such as fascia. **Ferris-Smith forceps** are heavy, long forceps with **large teeth** for **thick, dense tissue** such as fascia in deep wounds. Choosing the right forceps protects tissue from crush injury.',
match('Tissue forceps',[['Plain tissue forceps','Smooth tips for delicate tissue and dressings'],['Adson tissue forceps','Short and fine for superficial delicate tissue'],['Toothed Adson','1 × 2 teeth for grasping skin'],['Brown-Adson','Many fine interlocking teeth for gentle, secure grip'],['DeBakey','Atraumatic jaws for vessels and bowel'],['Bonney','Heavy toothed forceps for tough tissue'],['Ferris-Smith','Heavy, long, large-toothed forceps for dense tissue']])]],
['Skill I strategy',[
'Skill I has **15 numbered items** (photos or real instruments) in **15 minutes**: **1 point for the name, 1 for correct spelling and 1 for the purpose** (45 points). Learn the HOSA list exactly—including spelling of names such as **Metzenbaum**, **DeBakey**, **Halsted**, **Rochester-Pean**, **Kocher-Ochsner**, **Richardson-Eastman** and **Schnidt**. For purpose, give a short, specific use: “Metzenbaum scissors—dissecting delicate tissue,” not just “cutting.”',
'Identify by **features**: look at the **tips** (teeth, serrations, fenestrations), **curvature**, **length**, **ratchets** and **ring size**. Practice with flashcards and real instruments if your school has a tray. Work through all 15 items quickly, then return to uncertain ones.',
"Care of instruments matters too: use each instrument only for its intended purpose (scissors on tissue, not on wire or drapes), keep instruments free of blood during the case, open ratchets and box locks before processing, protect delicate tips and report damaged or misaligned instruments so they can be repaired.",
flip('Spelling to master',[['Metzenbaum','Delicate-tissue scissors'],['DeBakey','Atraumatic tissue forceps'],['Halsted mosquito','Fine hemostat for small vessels'],['Rochester-Pean','Large hemostat for heavy tissue'],['Kocher-Ochsner','Clamp with teeth at the tip'],['Richardson-Eastman','Double-ended hand-held retractor']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Choosing instruments',{a:{text:'The surgeon is about to make a small, precise skin incision on the face. Which blade and handle fit? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['No. 15 blade on a No. 3 handle','b','Correct. Small curved blade for precision.',true],['No. 20 blade on a No. 4 handle','b','That is a large blade for large incisions.',false]]},b:{text:'Next the surgeon needs to cut a suture. Which scissors?',choices:[['Straight Mayo scissors','c','Correct. Suture scissors.',true],['Metzenbaum scissors','c','Cutting sutures dulls Metzenbaums.',false]]},c:{text:'You pass each instrument correctly and keep the Metzenbaums for tissue.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Ringed instruments have rings, shanks, ratchet, box lock and jaws; classify instruments as cutting, grasping, clamping, retracting, suturing, suctioning and viewing.','No. 3 (and No. 3 long and No. 7) handles take blades 10, 11, 12 and 15; No. 4 takes 20–25; No. 10 for skin, No. 15 for precise incisions, No. 20 for large incisions; Beaver for mini blades; load blades with a needle holder.','Straight Mayo cuts sutures, curved Mayo cuts heavy tissue, Metzenbaum dissects delicate tissue; plain, Adson, toothed Adson, Brown-Adson, DeBakey (atraumatic), Bonney and Ferris-Smith (heavy toothed) forceps.'],terms:[['Box lock','The joint where the two halves of a ringed instrument meet.'],['Metzenbaum scissors','Fine scissors for dissecting delicate tissue.'],['DeBakey forceps','Atraumatic tissue forceps for vessels and delicate tissue.']],mcqs:[q('Which blades fit a No. 3 knife handle?','10, 11, 12 and 15','20–25','Beaver mini blades only','None','Small blades.'),q('Which blade is used for large skin incisions?','No. 10','No. 15','No. 11','No. 12','Large curved edge.'),q('Which scissors are used to cut sutures?','Straight Mayo','Metzenbaum','Iris','Potts','Suture scissors.'),q('Which scissors are used for delicate tissue dissection?','Metzenbaum','Straight Mayo','Bandage','Wire scissors','Never for sutures.'),q('Which forceps are atraumatic and used for vessels?','DeBakey','Bonney','Ferris-Smith','Toothed Adson','Fine longitudinal grooves.'),q('Brown-Adson forceps have:','Many fine interlocking teeth','No teeth','One large tooth','Fenestrated jaws','Often 7 × 7.'),q('The part of a hemostat that locks it closed is the:','Ratchet','Box lock','Shank','Jaw','Clicks in steps.'),q('How should a scalpel blade be loaded?','With a needle holder','With fingers','With tissue forceps','With scissors','Prevents injury.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would identify and state the purpose of six instruments from the HOSA Skill I list: a No. 3 knife handle, a No. 15 blade, curved Mayo scissors, Metzenbaum scissors, DeBakey tissue forceps and Ferris-Smith tissue forceps.',rubric:[['Knife handle and blade',['no. 3','10','11','12','15','small curved','precise']],['Scissors',['curved mayo','heavy','fascia','muscle','metzenbaum','delicate','not suture']],['Forceps',['debakey','atraumatic','vessels','ferris-smith','heavy','teeth','dense','fascia']],['Identification strategy',['tips','curvature','length','teeth','serrations','spelling','purpose']]],model:'A No. 3 knife handle is a flat, standard-length handle that accepts small blades—Nos. 10, 11, 12 and 15—and is used for most superficial incisions. A No. 15 blade has a small, curved cutting edge, so it is used for short, precise incisions such as on the face or around small vessels. Curved Mayo scissors are heavy scissors with curved, blunt blades, used to cut and dissect tough tissue such as fascia, muscle and the uterus. Metzenbaum scissors are longer and lighter with fine curved blades for dissecting delicate tissue, and they are never used to cut sutures. DeBakey tissue forceps are long thumb forceps with atraumatic jaws and fine longitudinal grooves that grip blood vessels and bowel without crushing them. Ferris-Smith tissue forceps are heavy, long thumb forceps with large teeth for grasping thick, dense tissue such as fascia in deep wounds. To identify instruments quickly, I look at the tips for teeth, serrations or smooth jaws, the curvature, the length and weight and whether there are rings and a ratchet, then write the name with correct spelling and a specific purpose, because Skill I awards points for name, spelling and purpose.'},sources:[RULES,AST,AST_STD]};

export const G7={title:'Instruments II: clamps, retractors, equipment and passing (Skills I and V)',hook:'The surgeon holds out a hand without looking up—and the right clamp, closed and pointed the right way, lands in the palm with a gentle snap. Passing instruments well means knowing the instruments, the surgeon’s hand signals and safe technique. This lesson completes the Skill I list and covers Skill V.',objectives:['Identify hemostatic clamps and grasping clamps by their jaws and uses.','Identify hand-held and self-retaining retractors.','Use hand signals and pass ringed instruments, retractors, sponge sticks and scalpels following HOSA Skill V.','Describe common OR equipment and supplies.'],sections:[
['Hemostatic clamps',[
'Hemostats clamp blood vessels and tissue. The **Halsted mosquito** is small and fine for **small vessels** and delicate tissue. The **Crile** has **horizontal serrations along the full length of the jaws** and is used for **medium vessels**; the **Kelly** looks similar but has serrations only on the **distal half** of the jaws. The **Rochester-Pean** is a **large, heavy** clamp with full horizontal serrations for **large vessels and tissue bundles**. The **Mixter** (right-angle) clamp has jaws bent at about 90° for **dissecting around** vessels and ducts and **passing ligatures** behind them.',
'The **Carmalt** has **longitudinal serrations** with cross-hatched tips and is used to clamp **pedicles and large vascular bundles** (for example, during hysterectomy) because the tissue can be ligated beneath it easily. The **Schnidt tonsil clamp** is a long, curved, slender clamp for **deep vessels** and passing ties, originally designed for tonsillectomy. The **Kocher-Ochsner** clamp has **horizontal serrations with 1 × 2 teeth at the tip**; its teeth grip **tough, heavy tissue** such as fascia and it is not used on vessels that will be saved.',
match('Clamp and use',[['Halsted mosquito','Small vessels'],['Crile','Medium vessels; serrations full length'],['Kelly','Serrations on the distal half only'],['Rochester-Pean','Large vessels and tissue bundles'],['Mixter','Right angle for dissecting and passing ligatures'],['Carmalt','Longitudinal serrations for pedicles'],['Schnidt tonsil','Deep vessels; passing ties'],['Kocher-Ochsner','Teeth at the tip for tough tissue']])]],
['Grasping and occluding clamps',[
'**Allis clamps** have short, multiple **interlocking teeth** that grasp firmly; they are considered somewhat **traumatic** and are used on tissue that is tough or will be removed. **Babcock clamps** have rounded, **fenestrated (open) atraumatic** jaws for grasping **delicate tubular structures**—**bowel**, the **appendix** and **fallopian tubes**—without crushing. **Doyen bowel clamps** are long, **non-crushing intestinal clamps** with flexible longitudinal jaws that occlude the bowel during resection and anastomosis.',
'Remember the pattern: **teeth = firm grip on tough tissue** (Allis, Kocher), **fenestrated and smooth = gentle grip on delicate tissue** (Babcock), **long flexible non-crushing jaws = bowel occlusion** (Doyen).',
sort('Traumatic or atraumatic?',['Grips firmly with teeth (more traumatic)','Atraumatic or non-crushing'],[['Allis',0],['Babcock',1],['Kocher-Ochsner',0],['Doyen bowel clamp',1],['DeBakey forceps',1],['Bonney forceps',0]])]],
['Retractors and equipment',[
'**Hand-held retractors** are held by an assistant: the **Richardson-Eastman** is **double-ended** with two blade sizes for retracting the **abdominal wall** and other layers; the **Deaver** has a smooth, **curved** blade for **deep abdominal and chest** retraction; the **Harrington (“sweetheart”)** has a large, **heart-shaped** blade for deep retraction of the **upper abdomen**—liver, diaphragm and spleen. **Self-retaining retractors** hold themselves open: the **Weitlaner** has rake-like prongs and a ratchet for small superficial incisions; the **O’Connor-O’Sullivan** and **Balfour** are large frames that hold the **abdominal wall** open (the Balfour often adds a bladder blade).',
'Other equipment: the **electrosurgical pencil** (active electrode handpiece for cutting and coagulating); **suction tips** (Yankauer for general suction, Frazier for fine areas, Poole for abdominal fluid); **sponges** (4 × 4s, laparotomy “lap” pads, Kittners/peanuts), always **radiopaque** for X-ray detection; **drains** (Jackson-Pratt, Penrose, Hemovac); and **catheters** (Foley).',
match('Retractor and use',[['Richardson-Eastman','Double-ended hand-held retractor'],['Deaver','Curved hand-held for deep abdominal retraction'],['Harrington (sweetheart)','Heart-shaped blade for the upper abdomen'],['Weitlaner','Small self-retaining retractor with rake prongs'],['Balfour','Self-retaining abdominal retractor'],['O’Connor-O’Sullivan','Self-retaining abdominal retractor'],['Electrosurgical pencil','Cuts and coagulates tissue'],['Yankauer','General suction tip']])]],
['Passing instruments: Skill V',[
'Skill V has 6 steps in **5 minutes**. **1.** Demonstrate hand signals for **hemostat, forceps, scissors, suture and scalpel**. Commonly used signals: **scalpel**—hand held palm down as if holding a pencil; **scissors**—index and middle fingers extended and moved like blades; **forceps**—thumb and index finger pinching together; **hemostat**—open hand, palm up, with a slight wrist turn; **suture/tie**—a hand motion as if tying, or an open hand for a loaded needle holder. Learn the signals your instructor and the HOSA scenario use.',
'**2. Ringed instruments:** make sure they are **locked** (closed on the first ratchet), hold them by the **box lock** and place the **rings in the surgeon’s open palm** with a **quick snap of the wrist**, oriented so the curve and tip face the way the surgeon will use them. **3. Position:** if the surgeon is on the **same side and to your left**, pass with your **right hand**; if to your **right**, pass with your **left hand**; if **across** the table, pass to the surgeon’s right or left hand based on the surgeon’s **dominant hand** (the judge will say which). **4. Retractors:** place the **handle** in the surgeon’s hand with the **blade pointing toward the incision**. **5. Sponge stick:** pass it with the sponge loaded, ready for immediate use. **6. Scalpel:** use the **no-hands technique** with a **neutral zone**—place the scalpel there, the surgeon retrieves and returns it. If hand-passing, hold the scalpel on top above the blade with the **blade facing you** and down toward the table, protect the blade with thumb and index finger, place the handle securely in the surgeon’s hand and move your hand **up and away** once the surgeon has it.',
order('Passing a ringed instrument',['Recognize the surgeon’s hand signal','Lock the instrument on the first ratchet','Hold it by the box lock','Orient the tips the way the surgeon will use them','Place the rings in the surgeon’s palm with a quick snap','Release when the surgeon has control'])]],
['Anticipation and the neutral zone',[
'Great scrubs **anticipate**: know the procedure’s steps, watch the field and have the next instrument ready—a hemostat before the surgeon cuts a vessel, scissors after a tie is placed, suture before closure. Keep the Mayo stand organized and return instruments promptly, wiping them with a moist sponge. Speak clearly when passing sharps (“**sharp**” or “**knife**”) so everyone knows where they are.',
'The **neutral zone**—a magnetic mat, basin or designated area on the field—reduces sharps injuries because two people never hold the same sharp at once. OSHA and AST support its use. Count every sharp and keep needles on a needle counter.',
kase('Passing practice',{a:{text:'The surgeon stands across the table and the judge says the surgeon is right-handed. The surgeon signals for a hemostat. How do you pass it? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Lock it, hold the box lock and snap the rings into the surgeon’s right palm with the curve oriented for use','b','Correct. Pass to the dominant hand, ready to use.',true],['Hand it over open, tips first','b','Rings go into the palm and the instrument is locked.',false]]},b:{text:'Next the surgeon asks for the knife. What is the safest method?',choices:[['Place it in the neutral zone for the surgeon to pick up','c','Correct. No-hands passing prevents injuries.',true],['Toss it gently across the table','c','Never toss sharps.',false]]},c:{text:'The surgeon returns the knife to the neutral zone, and you announce “sharp” as you retrieve it.',end:true}})]],
["Supplies and specialty equipment",["**Sponges** include **4 × 4 Raytec** sponges, **laparotomy pads** (lap sponges) for abdominal cases and small dissecting sponges (**Kittners** or peanuts, cottonoids for neurosurgery); all contain a **radiopaque marker**. **Drains** remove fluid and air: passive **Penrose** drains and closed suction drains such as **Jackson-Pratt** and **Hemovac**. **Catheters** include the **Foley** (indwelling urinary) catheter.","**Endoscopic** equipment includes the **laparoscope**, **light source**, **camera**, **insufflator** (CO₂), **trocars** and long instruments; handle scopes gently and keep light cords off drapes to prevent burns and fires. **Staplers** (skin, linear cutting, circular) speed closure and anastomosis. **Microscopes** and **loupes** support fine surgery.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Supply and use","pairs":[["Lap sponge","Large sponge for abdominal cases"],["Kittner","Small dissecting sponge"],["Jackson-Pratt drain","Closed suction drain"],["Penrose drain","Soft passive drain"],["Insufflator","Creates CO₂ pneumoperitoneum"],["Trocar","Port for laparoscopic instruments"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Hemostats: Halsted mosquito (small), Crile (full serrations), Kelly (distal half), Rochester-Pean (large), Mixter (right angle), Carmalt (longitudinal for pedicles), Schnidt tonsil (deep); Kocher-Ochsner has teeth; Allis grips tough tissue; Babcock is atraumatic for bowel and tubes; Doyen occludes bowel.','Retractors: Richardson-Eastman (double-ended), Deaver (curved deep), Harrington (sweetheart, upper abdomen); self-retaining Weitlaner, O’Connor-O’Sullivan and Balfour; electrosurgical pencil, suction tips, radiopaque sponges and drains.','Skill V: hand signals; lock ringed instruments, hold the box lock and snap rings into the palm; pass by the surgeon’s position and dominant hand; retractor handle first with the blade toward the incision; sponge stick ready; scalpel by neutral zone or with the blade protected and facing you.'],terms:[['Self-retaining retractor','A retractor that holds a wound open without being held.'],['Babcock clamp','An atraumatic fenestrated clamp for bowel and tubes.'],['Neutral zone','A designated area for placing and retrieving sharps.']],mcqs:[q('Weitlaner, O’Connor-O’Sullivan and Balfour are examples of:','Self-retaining retractors','Bone-holding forceps','Hand-held retractors','Non-crushing clamps','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which clamp has serrations only on the distal half of the jaws?','Kelly','Crile','Rochester-Pean','Carmalt','Crile is full length.'),q('Which clamp has jaws bent at a right angle?','Mixter','Allis','Babcock','Doyen','Dissecting and passing ties.'),q('Which clamp is atraumatic for bowel and fallopian tubes?','Babcock','Allis','Kocher-Ochsner','Carmalt','Fenestrated jaws.'),q('The Harrington retractor is nicknamed the:','Sweetheart','Rake','Ribbon','Army-Navy','Heart-shaped blade.'),q('How is a ringed instrument passed?','Locked, held by the box lock, rings snapped into the palm','Open, tips first','By the tips','Tossed','Ready for use.'),q('How is a hand-held retractor passed?','Handle to the surgeon with the blade toward the incision','Blade first','Upside down','Only by the circulator','Ready to place.'),q('The safest way to pass a scalpel is:','The no-hands neutral zone technique','Blade first into the palm','Tossing','Holding the blade','Reduces sharps injuries.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to pass a ringed instrument, a hand-held retractor and a scalpel following HOSA Skill V, including how the surgeon’s position changes which hand you use.',rubric:[['Ringed instruments',['locked','box lock','rings','palm','snap','curve']],['Positioning',['same side','left','right hand','right','left hand','across','dominant']],['Retractor and sponge stick',['handle','blade','incision','sponge stick','immediate use']],['Scalpel',['neutral zone','no-hands','on top','blade facing','protect','thumb','index','up and away']]],model:'When the surgeon signals for a ringed instrument such as a hemostat, I make sure it is locked on the first ratchet, hold it by the box lock and place the rings into the surgeon’s open palm with a quick snap of the wrist, with the curve and tips oriented the way the surgeon will use it. My passing hand depends on position: if the surgeon is on my side of the table and to my left, I pass with my right hand; if the surgeon is on my side and to my right, I pass with my left hand; and if the surgeon is across the table, I pass to the surgeon’s right or left hand according to the dominant hand the judge identifies. For a hand-held retractor, I place the handle in the surgeon’s hand with the blade pointing toward the incision, and I pass a sponge stick already loaded with a sponge so it is ready for immediate use. For the scalpel, the preferred method is the no-hands technique: I place it in the neutral zone, the surgeon picks it up, uses it and returns it there. If I hand-pass, I hold the scalpel on top above the blade with the blade facing me and toward the table, protect the blade with my thumb and index finger, place the handle securely in the surgeon’s hand and move my hand up and away once the surgeon has it.'},sources:[RULES,AST,OSHA_BBP]};

export const G8={title:'Hemostasis, wound healing and wound closure (Skill VI)',hook:'Every incision cuts blood vessels and every operation ends with closing a wound. Controlling bleeding, choosing the right suture and needle and understanding how wounds heal determine whether the patient recovers smoothly or develops a hematoma, infection or dehiscence. Hemostasis, wound healing and wound closure is 10% of the test, and Skill VI tests loading and passing sutures.',objectives:['Describe mechanical, thermal and chemical methods of hemostasis.','Classify surgical wounds and explain healing by first, second and third intention.','Compare absorbable and nonabsorbable sutures, suture sizes and needle types.','Load and pass a suture on a needle holder following HOSA Skill VI.'],sections:[
['Hemostasis methods',[
'**Mechanical** methods include **pressure** with sponges, **hemostatic clamps** followed by **ligatures** (ties), **ligating clips**, **staples**, **suture ligatures** (a stitch through tissue around a vessel) and **bone wax** (pressed into bone to stop bleeding). **Thermal** methods include **electrosurgery** (monopolar and bipolar), **lasers**, ultrasonic devices and vessel-sealing devices. **Chemical (pharmacologic)** methods include **topical thrombin**, **absorbable gelatin sponge** (Gelfoam), **oxidized regenerated cellulose** (Surgicel), **microfibrillar collagen** (Avitene), fibrin sealants and **epinephrine** in local anesthetics, which causes vasoconstriction.',
'A **free tie** is a strand of suture handed to the surgeon to tie around a clamped vessel; a **tie on a pass** is a tie clamped in the tip of an instrument so it can be passed around a deep vessel. Topical agents are labeled and handled carefully on the field.',
sort('Hemostasis method type',['Mechanical','Thermal','Chemical'],[['Ligating clip',0],['Bipolar cautery',1],['Topical thrombin',2],['Bone wax',0],['Laser',1],['Oxidized regenerated cellulose',2]])]],
['Wound classification and healing',[
'Surgical wounds are classified by contamination risk: **Class I (clean)**—no inflammation and no entry into the respiratory, GI or genitourinary tracts (for example, hernia repair); **Class II (clean-contaminated)**—controlled entry into those tracts (cholecystectomy, appendectomy without inflammation); **Class III (contaminated)**—open, fresh traumatic wounds, major breaks in sterile technique or gross spillage from the GI tract; **Class IV (dirty-infected)**—old traumatic wounds, existing infection or perforated viscera.',
'Wounds heal by **first intention (primary union)**—edges brought together with sutures or staples, minimal scarring; **second intention (granulation)**—the wound is left open to fill in from the bottom with granulation tissue, as in infected wounds or pressure injuries; and **third intention (delayed primary closure)**—the wound is left open for several days to clear infection, then closed. Healing passes through the **inflammatory**, **proliferative** and **maturation (remodeling)** phases. Complications include **infection**, **hematoma**, **seroma**, **dehiscence** (separation of wound layers) and **evisceration** (organs protruding through the wound—cover with sterile saline dressings and get help).',
match('Wound class',[['Inguinal hernia repair','Class I (clean)'],['Elective cholecystectomy','Class II (clean-contaminated)'],['Fresh traumatic laceration','Class III (contaminated)'],['Perforated appendix with pus','Class IV (dirty-infected)'],['Granulation from the bottom up','Second intention'],['Left open, then closed days later','Third intention']])]],
['Sutures',[
'**Absorbable sutures** are broken down by the body: **plain gut** and **chromic gut** (collagen; chromic lasts longer), **polyglactin 910** (Vicryl, braided), **poliglecaprone 25** (Monocryl, monofilament) and **polydioxanone** (PDS, long-lasting monofilament). They are used for internal layers and tissues that heal quickly. **Nonabsorbable sutures** remain unless removed: **silk** (braided, easy to handle), **nylon** (monofilament, often for skin), **polypropylene** (Prolene, monofilament, used in vascular surgery and hernia repair), **polyester** and **stainless steel** (sternum closure).',
'**Monofilament** sutures pass smoothly through tissue and harbor fewer bacteria; **multifilament (braided)** sutures handle and tie easily but can wick fluid. **Suture size** uses the USP system: the more zeros, the **finer** the suture—**6-0** is finer than **2-0**; sizes above 0 (1, 2) are heavier. Fine sutures (6-0 to 10-0) suit the face, eye and vessels; heavier sutures (0 to 2) suit fascia and high-tension areas. Alternatives include **staples**, **skin adhesive** and **adhesive strips**.',
"Suture **packets** show the material, size, length, needle type and needle size; the CST reads the packet aloud with the circulator and opens sutures in the order the surgeon will use them, keeping a few extras ready without wasting supplies. Tie lengths and reels are prepared for free ties.",
sort('Absorbable or nonabsorbable?',['Absorbable','Nonabsorbable'],[['Chromic gut',0],['Polyglactin 910 (Vicryl)',0],['Silk',1],['Polypropylene (Prolene)',1],['Polydioxanone (PDS)',0],['Nylon',1]]),
chk('Which suture is finest?',['6-0','2-0','0','2'],'More zeros means a finer suture.')]],
['Needles',[
'Surgical needles have three parts: the **eye or swage** (most are **swaged**, meaning permanently attached to the suture), the **body** and the **point**. **Taper-point** needles pierce and spread tissue without cutting—used for **soft tissue** such as bowel, vessels and muscle. **Cutting** needles have a sharp edge on the inner curve and **reverse cutting** needles have it on the outer curve—used for **tough tissue** such as **skin** and tendon (reverse cutting reduces tissue tear-out). **Blunt** needles are used for **friable tissue** such as liver and kidney and reduce needlestick risk.',
'Needles vary in **curvature** (¼, ⅜, ½ and ⅝ circle) and size. Needles are always handled with instruments, counted and kept on a needle counter. A **“pop-off”** needle releases from the suture with a gentle tug, useful for interrupted sutures.',
match('Needle and tissue',[['Taper point','Bowel and blood vessels'],['Reverse cutting','Skin'],['Blunt','Liver and kidney'],['Swaged needle','Permanently attached to the suture'],['Pop-off needle','Releases with a gentle tug'],['Needle counter','Keeps used needles accounted for']])]],
['Loading and passing sutures: Skill VI',[
'Skill VI has 7 steps in **4 minutes**. **1.** Load the needle at the **tip of the needle holder’s jaws** (commonly about one-third to one-half of the way from the swaged end, so the needle is held securely without bending). **2.** Set it at a **90-degree angle** to the long axis of the needle holder, pointing in the correct direction for **right- or left-handed** suturing (for a right-handed surgeon, the point usually faces left as you look at the jaws with the box lock toward you). **3.** Make sure the **box lock is fully locked** before passing.',
'**4.** Treat the needle holder as a **ringed instrument**: grasp it by the box lock and use a **quick snap of the wrist** to place the **rings in the surgeon’s palm**. **5.** Before passing, **drape the suture over the back of your hand** so it does not enter the surgeon’s hand with the instrument. **6.** **Control long sutures** until the surgeon has full control so the strand does not drag off the field and become contaminated. **7.** Maintain a **neutral zone** to minimize sharps injuries.',
order('Skill VI sequence',['Load the needle at the tip of the jaws','Set the needle at 90° for the surgeon’s hand','Lock the box lock fully','Drape the suture over the back of your hand','Grasp the box lock and snap the rings into the surgeon’s palm','Control the suture strand until the surgeon has it','Use the neutral zone for sharps'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Closure choices',{a:{text:'The surgeon is closing the abdominal fascia and asks for a strong, long-lasting absorbable monofilament suture. Which fits? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Polydioxanone (PDS) on a taper or appropriate needle','b','Correct. PDS is a long-lasting absorbable monofilament.',true],['Plain gut 6-0','b','Plain gut is fine and absorbs quickly.',false]]},b:{text:'For the skin, the surgeon wants a nonabsorbable monofilament on a needle that cuts tough tissue. Which do you load?',choices:[['Nylon on a reverse cutting needle','c','Correct. Reverse cutting needles suit skin.',true],['Silk on a blunt needle','c','Blunt needles are for friable organs.',false]]},c:{text:'You load each needle at the tip of the jaws at 90°, drape the strand over your hand and pass it ready to use.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Hemostasis is mechanical (pressure, clamps, ties, clips, bone wax), thermal (ESU, laser) or chemical (thrombin, gelatin sponge, oxidized cellulose, collagen, epinephrine).','Wounds are Class I clean, II clean-contaminated, III contaminated and IV dirty-infected; healing by first, second or third intention; watch for dehiscence and evisceration.','Absorbable (gut, Vicryl, Monocryl, PDS) vs nonabsorbable (silk, nylon, Prolene, steel); more zeros = finer; taper for soft tissue, cutting/reverse cutting for skin, blunt for liver; Skill VI: tip of the jaws at 90°, locked, suture over the back of your hand, rings into the palm, control the strand, neutral zone.'],terms:[['Swaged needle','A needle permanently attached to the suture strand.'],['Dehiscence','Separation of the layers of a surgical wound.'],['Ligature','A strand of suture tied around a vessel to stop bleeding.']],mcqs:[q('Which suture size is finest?','6-0','2-0','0','1','More zeros, finer.'),q('Which suture is absorbable?','Polyglactin 910 (Vicryl)','Silk','Nylon','Polypropylene','Braided absorbable.'),q('Which needle is best for skin?','Reverse cutting','Taper point','Blunt','None','Tough tissue.'),q('Which needle is used for liver tissue?','Blunt','Reverse cutting','Cutting','Trocar','Friable tissue.'),q('An elective cholecystectomy is which wound class?','Class II (clean-contaminated)','Class I','Class III','Class IV','Controlled entry into the GI tract.'),q('Healing from the bottom up with granulation tissue is:','Second intention','First intention','Third intention','Primary union','Open wound.'),q('In Skill VI, the needle is set at what angle to the needle holder?','90 degrees','45 degrees','180 degrees','30 degrees','Per the sheet.'),q('Before passing a loaded needle holder, the suture should be:','Draped over the back of your hand','Wrapped around the surgeon’s wrist','Cut short','Left on the floor','Keeps it from entering the surgeon’s hand.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare absorbable and nonabsorbable sutures and needle types, then explain how to load and pass a suture following HOSA Skill VI.',rubric:[['Suture types',['absorbable','gut','vicryl','monocryl','pds','nonabsorbable','silk','nylon','prolene']],['Size and structure',['zeros','finer','monofilament','braided','multifilament']],['Needles',['taper','soft tissue','cutting','reverse cutting','skin','blunt','liver','swaged']],['Skill VI',['tip','90','locked','box lock','rings','palm','back of your hand','control','neutral zone']]],model:'Absorbable sutures are broken down by the body and are used for internal tissues that heal quickly; examples are plain and chromic gut, polyglactin 910 (Vicryl), poliglecaprone (Monocryl) and long-lasting polydioxanone (PDS). Nonabsorbable sutures stay in the body or are removed later; examples are silk, nylon, polypropylene (Prolene) and stainless steel. Monofilament sutures glide through tissue and harbor fewer bacteria, while braided sutures tie easily. Sizes follow the USP system, in which more zeros means a finer suture—6-0 is finer than 2-0. Taper-point needles spread soft tissue such as bowel and vessels, cutting and reverse cutting needles penetrate tough tissue such as skin, and blunt needles are used for friable organs such as the liver; most needles are swaged to the suture. To load and pass a suture in Skill VI, I clamp the needle at the tip of the needle holder’s jaws, set it at a 90-degree angle to the holder and point it in the correct direction for the surgeon’s right- or left-handed technique, and lock the box lock fully. I drape the suture strand over the back of my hand so it does not go into the surgeon’s hand, grasp the needle holder by the box lock and snap the rings into the surgeon’s palm, control a long strand until the surgeon has it so it is not contaminated and use the neutral zone to prevent sharps injuries.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_STITCH,MEDLINE_WOUND,CDC_SSI]};

export const G9={title:'Surgical anatomy I: abdominal wall, digestive and genitourinary systems',hook:'To pass the right instrument at the right moment, a surgical technologist must know what the surgeon is cutting through and what lies beneath. Every layer of the abdominal wall, every organ and every capsule has a name. HOSA’s sample question asks for the capsule that covers the liver. Surgical anatomy and physiology is 20% of the test.',objectives:['Name the layers of the abdominal wall and common abdominal incisions.','Describe the anatomy of the digestive organs and common procedures.','Describe the kidney, urinary tract and reproductive anatomy relevant to surgery.','Match organ capsules and structures to their locations.'],sections:[
['Abdominal wall layers and incisions',[
'From outside in, the abdominal wall layers are: **skin**, **subcutaneous tissue** (fatty **Camper’s fascia** and membranous **Scarpa’s fascia**), the **anterior rectus sheath** (fascia), the **rectus abdominis muscle** (or the external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis laterally), the **posterior rectus sheath** (above the arcuate line), **transversalis fascia**, **preperitoneal fat** and the **peritoneum**. The **linea alba** is the fibrous midline where the rectus sheaths meet, and the **umbilicus** marks the T10 dermatome.',
'Common incisions: **vertical midline** (through the linea alba; fast, versatile exploratory laparotomy), **paramedian**, **right subcostal (Kocher)** for the gallbladder and liver, **McBurney (gridiron)** in the right lower quadrant for appendectomy, **Pfannenstiel** (low, curved transverse incision above the pubis) for cesarean birth and hysterectomy, **inguinal** for hernia repair and **flank** for the kidney. **Laparoscopic** surgery uses small ports and CO₂ **pneumoperitoneum**.',
order('Abdominal wall from outside in',['Skin','Subcutaneous tissue (Camper’s and Scarpa’s fascia)','Anterior rectus sheath','Rectus abdominis muscle','Posterior rectus sheath (above the arcuate line)','Transversalis fascia','Peritoneum']),
match('Incision and use',[['Vertical midline','Exploratory laparotomy'],['Right subcostal (Kocher)','Open gallbladder or liver surgery'],['McBurney','Open appendectomy'],['Pfannenstiel','Cesarean birth and hysterectomy'],['Inguinal','Hernia repair'],['Flank','Kidney surgery']])]],
['The liver, gallbladder and pancreas',[
'The **liver** is the largest internal organ, in the right upper quadrant under the diaphragm; it is covered by a fibrous and serous capsule called **Glisson’s capsule**. It has right and left lobes (plus caudate and quadrate), a dual blood supply from the **hepatic artery** and **portal vein**, and makes **bile**. The **falciform ligament** attaches it to the anterior abdominal wall. Liver tissue is **friable** and bleeds easily, so surgeons use blunt needles, topical hemostatics and vessel-sealing devices.',
'The **gallbladder** stores and concentrates bile under the liver; its **cystic duct** joins the **common hepatic duct** to form the **common bile duct**, which empties into the duodenum with the pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater. **Laparoscopic cholecystectomy** removes the gallbladder after the surgeon identifies the **cystic duct and cystic artery** (the “critical view of safety”) and clips them. The **pancreas** lies behind the stomach; it has exocrine (digestive enzymes) and endocrine (insulin, glucagon) functions. The **Whipple procedure** (pancreaticoduodenectomy) treats pancreatic head tumors.',
label('gi','The digestive system',[['liver','Liver','Covered by Glisson’s capsule.'],['gallbladder','Gallbladder','Removed in cholecystectomy.'],['pancreas','Pancreas','Behind the stomach; Whipple procedure.'],['stomach','Stomach','Gastrectomy and bariatric procedures.'],['smallint','Small intestine','Duodenum, jejunum and ileum.'],['largeint','Large intestine','Colectomy and colostomy.'],['appendix','Appendix','Removed in appendectomy.'],['esophagus','Esophagus','Fundoplication for reflux.']])]],
['Stomach, intestines and appendix',[
'The **stomach** has the cardia, fundus, body, antrum and pylorus; procedures include **gastrectomy**, **gastrostomy** tubes and **bariatric** operations such as sleeve gastrectomy and gastric bypass. The **small intestine** has three parts—**duodenum**, **jejunum** and **ileum**—and is suspended by the **mesentery**, which carries its blood supply. The **large intestine** includes the **cecum** (with the **appendix**), ascending, transverse, descending and sigmoid colon, rectum and anus.',
'**Bowel resections** remove diseased segments, often with **Doyen** non-crushing clamps and stapling devices, followed by an **anastomosis** (reconnection) or an **ostomy**. **Bowel technique** (isolation technique) separates instruments that have touched the open bowel lumen from clean instruments to reduce contamination. **Appendectomy** is usually laparoscopic; the appendix base is ligated or stapled. **Hernia** repairs (inguinal, femoral, umbilical, incisional) return protruding contents and reinforce the wall, often with **mesh**.',
"The **spleen** in the left upper quadrant is fragile and bleeds heavily when injured; **splenectomy** patients need vaccines afterward because the spleen helps fight encapsulated bacteria. The **peritoneum** lines the abdominal cavity (parietal layer) and covers organs (visceral layer); folds such as the **greater omentum** hang from the stomach and can wall off infection. **Retroperitoneal** organs—kidneys, pancreas, much of the duodenum and the aorta—lie behind the peritoneum.",
match('Digestive procedure',[['Cholecystectomy','Removal of the gallbladder'],['Appendectomy','Removal of the appendix'],['Colectomy','Removal of part of the colon'],['Anastomosis','Surgical reconnection of bowel'],['Herniorrhaphy','Repair of a hernia'],['Bowel technique','Isolating instruments that touched the open bowel']])]],
['Genitourinary and reproductive anatomy',[
'Each **kidney** lies retroperitoneally and is surrounded by a fibrous **renal capsule**, perirenal fat and **Gerota’s fascia**. Its functional units are **nephrons**; each begins with a **glomerulus** inside **Bowman’s capsule**, which collects the filtrate. Urine flows through the **renal pelvis** to the **ureter**, **bladder** and **urethra**. Procedures include **nephrectomy**, **cystoscopy** (viewing the bladder), **ureteroscopy** for stones and **transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP)**.',
'Female reproductive anatomy includes the **uterus**, **fallopian tubes**, **ovaries**, **cervix** and **vagina**; procedures include **hysterectomy** (abdominal, vaginal or laparoscopic), **cesarean section**, **dilation and curettage (D&C)** and **tubal ligation**. Male anatomy includes the **testes**, **epididymis**, **vas deferens**, **prostate** and **penis**; procedures include **vasectomy**, **orchiectomy** and **prostatectomy**. Note other capsules that appear on tests: **Tenon’s capsule** surrounds the eyeball, and an **articular capsule** encloses a joint.',
match('Capsule and location',[['Glisson’s capsule','Covers the liver'],['Bowman’s capsule','Surrounds the glomerulus in the kidney'],['Tenon’s capsule','Surrounds the eyeball'],['Articular capsule','Encloses a joint'],['Gerota’s fascia','Encloses the kidney and perirenal fat'],['Renal capsule','Fibrous covering of the kidney']]),
label('urinary','The urinary system',[['kidney','Kidney','Contains nephrons with Bowman’s capsules.'],['ureter','Ureter','Carries urine to the bladder.'],['bladder','Bladder','Viewed during cystoscopy.'],['urethra','Urethra','Route for TURP and catheters.']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Anatomy in the OR',{a:{text:'During a laparoscopic cholecystectomy, which two structures must the surgeon identify before clipping? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['The cystic duct and cystic artery','b','Correct. The critical view of safety prevents bile duct injury.',true],['The ureter and renal artery','b','Those are kidney structures.',false]]},b:{text:'In an open bowel resection, the surgeon finishes the anastomosis. What should happen to the instruments used inside the bowel?',choices:[['Isolate them from the clean setup using bowel technique','c','Correct. This reduces contamination of the wound.',true],['Use them to close the skin','c','Bowel instruments are contaminated.',false]]},c:{text:'You set the contaminated instruments aside, change gloves as directed and pass clean instruments for closure.',end:true}})]],
["Positioning and approach for abdominal surgery",["Most open abdominal procedures use the **supine** position. **Reverse Trendelenburg** (head up) helps upper abdominal and laparoscopic gallbladder surgery by letting the bowel fall away; **Trendelenburg** (head down) helps pelvic surgery. **Lithotomy** is used for vaginal procedures, cystoscopy and TURP, and the **lateral (kidney)** position with the kidney rest raised opens the flank for nephrectomy.","Laparoscopic cases start with entering the abdomen (with a Veress needle or open Hasson technique), insufflating with CO₂ to about 12–15 mm Hg and placing trocars under direct vision. The CST keeps the camera lens clean and anti-fogged, manages instrument exchanges and is ready to convert to an open procedure with a laparotomy tray if bleeding or other problems occur.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Best position","buckets":["Reverse Trendelenburg","Trendelenburg","Lithotomy","Lateral"],"items":[["Laparoscopic cholecystectomy",0],["Pelvic laparoscopy",1],["Cystoscopy",2],["Nephrectomy",3],["Vaginal hysterectomy",2],["Laparoscopic hysterectomy",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Abdominal wall: skin, subcutaneous (Camper’s and Scarpa’s), anterior rectus sheath, rectus muscle, posterior sheath, transversalis fascia, peritoneum; incisions include midline, Kocher subcostal, McBurney, Pfannenstiel, inguinal and flank.','Glisson’s capsule covers the liver; cystic duct + common hepatic duct = common bile duct; identify the cystic duct and artery in cholecystectomy; use bowel technique after opening bowel.','Kidney nephrons begin with the glomerulus in Bowman’s capsule; Tenon’s capsule surrounds the eye; procedures include nephrectomy, cystoscopy, TURP, hysterectomy, cesarean section and vasectomy.'],terms:[['Glisson’s capsule','The fibrous and serous capsule covering the liver.'],['Anastomosis','A surgical connection between two structures, such as bowel ends.'],['Pfannenstiel incision','A low, curved transverse incision above the pubis.']],mcqs:[q('What is the name of the fibrous and serous capsule that covers the liver?','Glisson’s','Bowman’s','Tenon’s','Articular','HOSA sample question.'),q('Bowman’s capsule is found in the:','Kidney','Liver','Eye','Knee','Surrounds the glomerulus.'),q('Which incision is used for an open appendectomy?','McBurney','Pfannenstiel','Kocher subcostal','Midline sternotomy','Right lower quadrant.'),q('The fibrous midline of the abdominal wall is the:','Linea alba','Arcuate line','Scarpa’s fascia','Peritoneum','Where the rectus sheaths meet.'),q('The cystic duct joins the common hepatic duct to form the:','Common bile duct','Pancreatic duct','Ureter','Portal vein','Empties into the duodenum.'),q('Which part of the small intestine comes first?','Duodenum','Jejunum','Ileum','Cecum','Then jejunum and ileum.'),q('A Pfannenstiel incision is commonly used for:','Cesarean section','Kidney removal','Thyroidectomy','Open heart surgery','Low transverse.'),q('Tenon’s capsule surrounds the:','Eyeball','Liver','Kidney','Spleen','Ophthalmic anatomy.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the layers the surgeon cuts through in a vertical midline laparotomy and explain the key anatomy and surgical steps of a laparoscopic cholecystectomy.',rubric:[['Layers',['skin','subcutaneous','scarpa','linea alba','fascia','transversalis','peritoneum']],['Biliary anatomy',['liver','glisson','gallbladder','cystic duct','common hepatic','common bile duct','cystic artery']],['Laparoscopic steps',['ports','pneumoperitoneum','co2','critical view','clips','divide','remove']],['Positioning and care',['reverse trendelenburg','supine','count','specimen','bile','instruments']]],model:'In a vertical midline laparotomy, the surgeon cuts through the skin and the subcutaneous tissue—fatty Camper’s fascia and membranous Scarpa’s fascia—then divides the linea alba, the fibrous midline where the rectus sheaths meet, so the rectus muscles are separated rather than cut, then the transversalis fascia and preperitoneal fat, and finally opens the peritoneum to enter the abdominal cavity. In a laparoscopic cholecystectomy, the patient lies supine and is often placed in reverse Trendelenburg with a slight tilt so the bowel falls away from the right upper quadrant. The surgeon places ports and creates a CO₂ pneumoperitoneum to see the gallbladder under the liver, which is covered by Glisson’s capsule. The gallbladder is retracted to expose the triangle containing the cystic duct, which joins the common hepatic duct to form the common bile duct, and the cystic artery. The surgeon achieves the critical view of safety by clearly identifying only the cystic duct and cystic artery entering the gallbladder, then clips and divides them, dissects the gallbladder off the liver bed with electrosurgery and removes it through a port, often in a bag. The CST anticipates the clip applier, cautery and specimen bag, handles the specimen and performs counts before closure.'},sources:[RULES,OSA_ABD,OSA_DIGEST,OSA_KIDNEY]};

export const G10={title:'Surgical anatomy II and patient care: cardiovascular, orthopedic, neuro and anesthesia',hook:'Surgical technologists scrub for everything from a hip replacement to open-heart surgery to brain tumor removal. Each specialty has its own anatomy, instruments and risks, and every patient goes through anesthesia, which brings emergencies such as malignant hyperthermia. This lesson completes the surgical anatomy and physiology section with specialty anatomy and perioperative patient care.',objectives:['Describe cardiovascular and thoracic anatomy relevant to surgery.','Describe orthopedic and neurosurgical anatomy and common procedures.','Describe the eye, ear, nose and throat anatomy relevant to surgery.','Explain types of anesthesia, malignant hyperthermia and perioperative care.'],sections:[
['Cardiovascular and thoracic surgery',[
'The **heart** lies in the **mediastinum**, surrounded by the **pericardium**, with four chambers and four valves (tricuspid, pulmonary, mitral and aortic). The **coronary arteries** supply heart muscle; blockages are bypassed in **coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG)** using the **great saphenous vein** or **internal mammary (thoracic) artery**. Open-heart surgery uses a **median sternotomy** (the sternum is split with a sternal saw and closed with **stainless steel wires**) and often **cardiopulmonary bypass** run by a **perfusionist**.',
'**Vascular** procedures include carotid endarterectomy, aortic aneurysm repair and arterial bypass, using fine atraumatic instruments (DeBakey forceps, vascular clamps), **heparin** and **polypropylene** sutures. **Thoracic** procedures (lobectomy, pneumonectomy) often use a **posterolateral thoracotomy** in the lateral position or **video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS)**, with **chest tubes** afterward to drain air and fluid.',
label('heart','The heart',[['aorta','Aorta','Clamped during cardiopulmonary bypass.'],['lv','Left ventricle','Main pumping chamber.'],['mitral','Mitral valve','Repaired or replaced in valve surgery.'],['aortic','Aortic valve','Replaced in aortic stenosis.'],['ra','Right atrium','Venous cannula for bypass.'],['svc','Superior vena cava','Returns blood from the upper body.']])]],
['Orthopedic surgery',[
'Bones have a dense **cortical** outer layer and **cancellous (spongy)** inner bone with marrow; the **periosteum** covers bone and contains blood vessels and nerves. Joints are enclosed in an **articular capsule** lined by synovium. Orthopedic procedures include **open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF)** with plates and screws, **intramedullary nails** for long bones, **total joint arthroplasty** (hip and knee replacement) and **arthroscopy** to view and repair joints through small incisions.',
'Orthopedic cases use power **drills, saws and reamers**, **bone-holding forceps**, **rongeurs**, **osteotomes**, **curettes** and **bone wax**. **Methyl methacrylate** bone cement requires fume control and monitoring for a drop in blood pressure. A **pneumatic tourniquet** creates a bloodless field; the time is tracked and reported. **Fluoroscopy** (C-arm) requires lead protection.',
label('joint','A synovial joint',[['capsule','Articular capsule','Encloses the joint.'],['synovium','Synovium','Lines the capsule.'],['cartilage','Articular cartilage','Replaced in arthroplasty.'],['bone','Cortical bone','Holds screws and plates.'],['spongy','Cancellous bone','Spongy inner bone.'],['marrow','Marrow','Inside the medullary canal where nails are placed.']])]],
['Neurosurgery and special senses',[
'The **brain** is protected by the skull and three **meninges**—**dura mater**, **arachnoid** and **pia mater**—with cerebrospinal fluid in the subarachnoid space. **Craniotomy** opens the skull with a drill and craniotome to remove tumors or blood clots; positioning may use a **skull clamp**. Spinal procedures such as **laminectomy** and **discectomy** relieve pressure on nerves, often in the **prone** position. Hemostasis in neurosurgery relies on **bipolar** cautery, cottonoids (patties) and gelatin sponge.',
'**Eye** surgery addresses the cornea, lens (**cataract extraction** with lens implant), retina and **Tenon’s capsule**; it uses microscopes and fine instruments such as **Beaver blades**. **Ear** surgery (tympanoplasty, myringotomy with tubes) uses microscopes. **Nose and throat** procedures include tonsillectomy (with the **Schnidt tonsil clamp**), septoplasty and endoscopic sinus surgery. **Thyroidectomy** requires protecting the **recurrent laryngeal nerve** and parathyroid glands.',
match('Specialty structure',[['Dura mater','Tough outer meninx opened in craniotomy'],['Lamina','Part of the vertebra removed in laminectomy'],['Lens','Replaced in cataract surgery'],['Tympanic membrane','Repaired in tympanoplasty'],['Recurrent laryngeal nerve','Protected during thyroidectomy'],['Great saphenous vein','Harvested for CABG']])]],
['Anesthesia and malignant hyperthermia',[
'**General anesthesia** produces unconsciousness with airway management (endotracheal tube or laryngeal mask airway) and has three phases: **induction**, **maintenance** and **emergence**. The OR should be **quiet during induction and emergence**. **Regional anesthesia** includes **spinal** and **epidural** blocks and peripheral nerve blocks; **monitored anesthesia care (MAC)** or moderate sedation keeps patients relaxed while breathing on their own; **local anesthesia** numbs a small area (lidocaine, sometimes with epinephrine for vasoconstriction).',
'**Malignant hyperthermia (MH)** is a rare, inherited, life-threatening reaction to **volatile inhalation anesthetics** and **succinylcholine**. Early signs are a **rising end-tidal CO₂**, tachycardia, **muscle rigidity** (including masseter spasm) and later a rapidly rising temperature. Treatment: stop triggering agents, hyperventilate with 100% oxygen, give **dantrolene** as quickly as possible, cool the patient and treat complications. Every OR keeps an **MH cart** stocked with dantrolene, and the CST may help mix it.',
sort('Anesthesia type',['General','Regional','Local or MAC'],[['Endotracheal tube and inhaled agents',0],['Spinal block for a cesarean section',1],['Lidocaine injection for a mole removal',2],['Epidural for knee replacement',1],['Sedation for a cataract procedure',2],['Laryngeal mask airway with unconsciousness',0]]),
order('Responding to malignant hyperthermia',['Recognize rising end-tidal CO₂, rigidity and tachycardia','Call for help and the MH cart','Stop volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine','Hyperventilate with 100% oxygen','Give dantrolene quickly','Cool the patient and monitor'])]],
['Perioperative patient care',[
'Before surgery, the team confirms **identity, procedure, site, consent, allergies, NPO status** and implants; the **time-out** confirms the plan. During surgery, the team maintains **normothermia** (warming blankets), positions and pads the patient, monitors fluid and blood loss, handles **specimens** correctly (labeled with the patient’s name, specimen type and side, in the right preservative such as formalin or sent fresh for frozen section) and performs counts. The CST handles specimens on the field and passes them to the circulator.',
'After surgery, the CST helps apply **dressings**, removes drapes carefully, assists with moving the patient to the stretcher and breaks down the setup safely—**sharps first**—and sends instruments for processing. The patient goes to the **post-anesthesia care unit (PACU)**, where staff monitor airway, vital signs, pain and nausea. Postoperative complications include bleeding, infection, deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism, atelectasis and pneumonia, which early ambulation and breathing exercises help prevent.',
kase('Intraoperative emergency',{a:{text:'Under general anesthesia with a volatile agent, a patient’s end-tidal CO₂ rises rapidly, the heart rate climbs and the jaw is rigid. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the likely problem?',choices:[['Malignant hyperthermia','b','Correct. Rising end-tidal CO₂ is an early sign.',true],['A normal response to surgery','b','These are warning signs.',false]]},b:{text:'What is the key drug, and how can the CST help?',choices:[['Dantrolene—bring the MH cart and help mix vials as directed','c','Correct. Fast dantrolene saves lives.',true],['More succinylcholine','c','Succinylcholine is a trigger.',false]]},c:{text:'The anesthesia team gives dantrolene, cooling begins and the case is ended safely. The event is documented.',end:true}})]],
["Specimens, counts and documentation",["Specimens are handled carefully because they can determine a diagnosis: keep each specimen on the field in a labeled container or basin, never let it dry out or be discarded, and announce it to the circulator with the **exact name and side**. Some go **fresh** for **frozen section** (rapid pathology during surgery), some in **formalin**, and some (such as cultures or stones) in special containers. The circulator labels and documents each specimen.","Count discrepancies, implant records (lot numbers), wound class, drains and dressings are documented in the intraoperative record. Accurate documentation and clear **handoff** to the PACU nurse (procedure, anesthesia, fluids, blood loss, drains, concerns) keep the patient safe after surgery.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Specimen handling","pairs":[["Frozen section","Sent fresh for rapid diagnosis"],["Routine tissue","Placed in formalin"],["Culture swab","Special culture container"],["Specimen label","Patient, specimen type and side"],["Implant","Lot number recorded"],["Handoff","Report to the PACU nurse"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Cardiac surgery uses median sternotomy (closed with steel wire), cardiopulmonary bypass and CABG with saphenous vein or internal mammary artery; vascular cases use atraumatic instruments, heparin and polypropylene.','Orthopedics: cortical and cancellous bone, ORIF, nails, arthroplasty, arthroscopy, bone cement, tourniquets and fluoroscopy; neurosurgery: meninges, craniotomy, laminectomy and bipolar cautery; eye, ear and throat procedures protect nerves and use microscopes.','Anesthesia: general, regional, MAC and local; malignant hyperthermia (rising end-tidal CO₂, rigidity, tachycardia; triggered by volatile agents and succinylcholine) is treated with dantrolene; handle specimens and counts carefully and break down sharps first.'],terms:[['Median sternotomy','A vertical incision through the sternum for heart surgery.'],['ORIF','Open reduction and internal fixation of a fracture.'],['Malignant hyperthermia','An inherited, life-threatening reaction to certain anesthetics.']],mcqs:[q('Which drug treats malignant hyperthermia?','Dantrolene','Succinylcholine','Heparin','Epinephrine','Keep it on the MH cart.'),q('An early sign of malignant hyperthermia is:','Rising end-tidal CO₂','Low heart rate','Muscle relaxation','Low temperature first','Temperature rises later.'),q('A median sternotomy is closed with:','Stainless steel wires','Plain gut','Skin glue only','Staples only','Strong closure.'),q('Which vessel is often harvested for CABG?','Great saphenous vein','Jugular vein','Femoral artery','Portal vein','Or the internal mammary artery.'),q('ORIF stands for:','Open reduction and internal fixation','Operating room internal fluid','Orthopedic rapid infusion fix','Outer radial immobilization','Fracture repair.'),q('Which meninx is the tough outer layer?','Dura mater','Pia mater','Arachnoid','Periosteum','Opened in craniotomy.'),q('A spinal block is which type of anesthesia?','Regional','General','Local','MAC','Nerve block.'),q('During breakdown of the setup, what is removed first?','Sharps','Drapes','Basins','Gowns','Safety first.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the three main types of anesthesia and explain how the surgical team recognizes and responds to malignant hyperthermia, including the CST’s role.',rubric:[['Anesthesia types',['general','induction','maintenance','emergence','regional','spinal','epidural','local','mac']],['MH triggers and signs',['volatile','succinylcholine','end-tidal','co2','rigidity','masseter','tachycardia','temperature']],['Treatment',['stop','100% oxygen','hyperventilate','dantrolene','cool','monitor']],['CST role',['mh cart','mix','quiet','assist','count','document','end the case']]],model:'General anesthesia makes the patient unconscious and requires airway management with an endotracheal tube or laryngeal mask airway; it has three phases—induction, maintenance and emergence—and the room should be quiet during induction and emergence. Regional anesthesia blocks sensation to part of the body, as with spinal or epidural anesthesia for a cesarean section or knee replacement, while the patient may be awake or sedated. Local anesthesia numbs a small area with an injected drug such as lidocaine, often combined with monitored anesthesia care or moderate sedation. Malignant hyperthermia is a rare inherited reaction triggered by volatile inhalation anesthetics and succinylcholine. The earliest sign is usually an unexplained rise in end-tidal CO₂, along with tachycardia, muscle rigidity such as masseter spasm, irregular heart rhythms and, later, a rapidly rising temperature. The team calls for help, stops the triggering agents, hyperventilates the patient with 100% oxygen, gives dantrolene as quickly as possible, cools the patient and treats acid-base and potassium problems. The CST helps by bringing the MH cart, mixing dantrolene vials with sterile water as directed, assisting the surgeon to finish or close quickly, maintaining the sterile field and counts and supporting documentation.'},sources:[RULES,ASA_ANES,MHAUS,AST]};
